\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces What sets $\mathrm  {EIRP}_{90}$, and in which direction. The uncertainty on the calibration is an uncertainty on the class-average factor, and so on any statistic formed over many windows; its flux term is band-dependent, so the submillimetre windows carry the larger combined figure. The spread between windows is an observed dispersion and is the statement about one window's own limit; the signed terms are biases, stated with their direction rather than folded into an error bar, and the decorrelation term is measured in 279{} of the 407{} blocks and not imputed to the rest.}}{12}{table.5.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:p90budget}{{4}{12}{What sets $\mathrm {EIRP}_{90}$, and in which direction. The uncertainty on the calibration is an uncertainty on the class-average factor, and so on any statistic formed over many windows; its flux term is band-dependent, so the submillimetre windows carry the larger combined figure. The spread between windows is an observed dispersion and is the statement about one window's own limit; the signed terms are biases, stated with their direction rather than folded into an error bar, and the decorrelation term is measured in \SensDecorNBlk {} of the \SensDecorNBlkTot {} blocks and not imputed to the rest}{table.5.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The two completenesses of the carrier search, over the same 61{} systems.} \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}Single-epoch trigger completeness: the cumulative number of systems whose best window reaches a given $\mathrm  {EIRP}_{90}$ (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:quantities}), evaluated as $3.03\,P_{\rm  trig}$ with each window's own primary-beam, annual-parallax and smearing retentions applied and atmospheric decorrelation excluded, which Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:persys} gives system by system; the shaded band is the calibration interval of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:p90budget}. \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}Confirmation completeness, which no amount of power buys: systems grouped by the longest separation between two execution blocks of different observing sessions covering a common frequency, which is what the criterion of \S  \ref  {sec:summary} requires, over the primary census together with every repeat execution block the recurrence test of \S  \ref  {sec:etacrv} reads. \textbf  {Left of the dashed line, 21{} systems have no such pair, and for them confirmation was not possible at any sensitivity in panel\nobreakspace  {}(a)}. The two panels are separate statements and neither follows from the other: (a) is the power at which a carrier crosses the trigger in one block, (b) is whether a crossing there could then have been tested at all. The limits of panel\nobreakspace  {}(a) are completeness for a single-epoch crossing and not for a confirmed detection, and for the 21{} systems left of the line no power would have bought one.}}{12}{figure.5.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:classasens}{{6}{12}{\textbf {The two completenesses of the carrier search, over the same \NSysClassA {} systems.} \emph {(a)}~Single-epoch trigger completeness: the cumulative number of systems whose best window reaches a given $\mathrm {EIRP}_{90}$ (Table~\ref {tab:quantities}), evaluated as $\EirpNinetyMultA \,P_{\rm trig}$ with each window's own primary-beam, annual-parallax and smearing retentions applied and atmospheric decorrelation excluded, which Table~\ref {tab:persys} gives system by system; the shaded band is the calibration interval of Table~\ref {tab:p90budget}. \emph {(b)}~Confirmation completeness, which no amount of power buys: systems grouped by the longest separation between two execution blocks of different observing sessions covering a common frequency, which is what the criterion of \S \ref {sec:summary} requires, over the primary census together with every repeat execution block the recurrence test of \S \ref {sec:etacrv} reads. \textbf {Left of the dashed line, \ZcNoRepSys {} systems have no such pair, and for them confirmation was not possible at any sensitivity in panel~(a)}. The two panels are separate statements and neither follows from the other: (a) is the power at which a carrier crosses the trigger in one block, (b) is whether a crossing there could then have been tested at all. The limits of panel~(a) are completeness for a single-epoch crossing and not for a confirmed detection, and for the \ZcNoRepSys {} systems left of the line no power would have bought one}{figure.5.6}{}}
