\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The strongest unattributed crossing of the quality-passing data, and the control field it leads.} Frequencies are in the star's rest frame. \emph  {(a)}\nobreakspace  {}The dynamic spectrum at 61 Vir's position around the crossing at 346.3232\,GHz, observed 2017 May 20, with the fitted drift of +3182\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ as the dashed track. The 225{} integrations are binned into 9{} and plotted in units of each bin's noise. At 119$\pm $20\,mJy against 303\,mJy per integration the carrier is 0.39{} standard deviations in any one of them, and exists only in the drift-aligned stack of all 38{} minutes on source. \emph  {(b)}\nobreakspace  {}The same rows shifted by that drift, which carries the carrier 22{} channels of 488.3\,kHz across the observation. \emph  {(c)}\nobreakspace  {}The de-drifted stack at the star (black) and at the 8{} control positions with retained dynamic spectra (grey), in units of its own noise; dashed, the trigger. \emph  {(d)}\nobreakspace  {}The same statistic at each of the 512{} rank-screen control positions: the star (solid) against the highest control (dotted). The recurrence test for this crossing, and for the second one -- CP$-$72 2713{} at 344.2965\,GHz -- is in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:cand}.}}{18}{figure.5.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:event}{{7}{18}{\textbf {The strongest unattributed crossing of the quality-passing data, and the control field it leads.} Frequencies are in the star's rest frame. \emph {(a)}~The dynamic spectrum at \EvAStar 's position around the crossing at \RqFreqSx \,GHz, observed \EvADate , with the fitted drift of \EvADrift \,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ as the dashed track. The \EvANInt {} integrations are binned into \EvANBin {} and plotted in units of each bin's noise. At \EvAFlux $\pm $\EvAFluxErr \,mJy against \EvARmsInt \,mJy per integration the carrier is \EvAPerIntSig {} standard deviations in any one of them, and exists only in the drift-aligned stack of all \EvADwellMin {} minutes on source. \emph {(b)}~The same rows shifted by that drift, which carries the carrier \EvADriftChan {} channels of \EvAChanwkHz \,kHz across the observation. \emph {(c)}~The de-drifted stack at the star (black) and at the \RcNRetCtrl {} control positions with retained dynamic spectra (grey), in units of its own noise; dashed, the trigger. \emph {(d)}~The same statistic at each of the \NCtrl {} rank-screen control positions: the star (solid) against the highest control (dotted). The recurrence test for this crossing, and for the second one -- \EvBStar {} at \RqFreqCp \,GHz -- is in Table~\ref {tab:cand}}{figure.5.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Can two crossings be one carrier?} Every pair of crossings toward one star, in two execution blocks, lying within $\pm 833$\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of each other in the star's frame -- the half-width of the median spectral window a repeat could be found in -- and carrying between them two independently fitted drifts and two block epochs measured from their own data: 13{} pairs with an unattributed member, of the 16{} inside that interval. Below the rule is the control, the 1{} of 23{} pairs of already attributed crossings for which a drift and an epoch were both recorded; a test that cannot return one carrier for it is not a test. Stars are identified by position, so the two archival-tail crossings of HD\nobreakspace  {}14055{} pair with its catalogued ones. $\Delta \nu $ is the stellar-frame offset and $\dot  \nu _1$, $\dot  \nu _2$ the drifts fitted independently in the two blocks and carried into that frame. A transmitter at constant line-of-sight acceleration would put the pair at $\Delta \nu =\frac  {1}{2}(\dot  \nu _1+\dot  \nu _2)\Delta t$, and \emph  {miss} is how far it is from that in units of the drift grid's resolution carried over $\Delta t$; a dash means that resolution exceeds the window searched, so the comparison has no power. The last column is the verdict under the weaker criterion of a trajectory whose drift stays inside the searched grid: \emph  {no} where no such trajectory joins the pair, \emph  {open} where the pair is not separable -- neither a confirmation nor an exclusion -- \emph  {yes} for the control row. The drifts are in the star's frame while the grid was stepped in the observatory's, and a fitted drift is refined inside one grid step, so a printed value can lie beyond the outermost trial of its own window. The largest here is 4184\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ as fitted, which is 0.72{} of a 59\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ step past that trial. It gains a further 32\,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ on conversion into the star's frame. The grid itself stops at the 12.0\,nHz ceiling of \S  \ref  {sec:method}; that window's outermost trial is 11.99\,nHz.}}{18}{table.5.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pairs}{{5}{18}{\textbf {Can two crossings be one carrier?} Every pair of crossings toward one star, in two execution blocks, lying within $\pm \WdHalfKms $\,km\,s$^{-1}$ of each other in the star's frame -- the half-width of the median spectral window a repeat could be found in -- and carrying between them two independently fitted drifts and two block epochs measured from their own data: \TjNPair {} pairs with an unattributed member, of the \TjNCandPair {} inside that interval. Below the rule is the control, the \TjNCandAttrTest {} of \TjNCandAttr {} pairs of already attributed crossings for which a drift and an epoch were both recorded; a test that cannot return one carrier for it is not a test. Stars are identified by position, so the two archival-tail crossings of \TjDeepStar {} pair with its catalogued ones. $\Delta \nu $ is the stellar-frame offset and $\dot \nu _1$, $\dot \nu _2$ the drifts fitted independently in the two blocks and carried into that frame. A transmitter at constant line-of-sight acceleration would put the pair at $\Delta \nu =\frac {1}{2}(\dot \nu _1+\dot \nu _2)\Delta t$, and \emph {miss} is how far it is from that in units of the drift grid's resolution carried over $\Delta t$; a dash means that resolution exceeds the window searched, so the comparison has no power. The last column is the verdict under the weaker criterion of a trajectory whose drift stays inside the searched grid: \emph {no} where no such trajectory joins the pair, \emph {open} where the pair is not separable -- neither a confirmation nor an exclusion -- \emph {yes} for the control row. The drifts are in the star's frame while the grid was stepped in the observatory's, and a fitted drift is refined inside one grid step, so a printed value can lie beyond the outermost trial of its own window. The largest here is \TjGridTopoHzS \,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ as fitted, which is \TjGridOverStep {} of a \TjGridStepHzS \,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ step past that trial. It gains a further \TjGridFrameHzS \,Hz\,s$^{-1}$ on conversion into the star's frame. The grid itself stops at the \DriftCeilLo \,nHz ceiling of \S \ref {sec:method}; that window's outermost trial is \TjGridEdgeNHz \,nHz}{table.5.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The two events}, the crossings whose stellar statistic exceeds all 512{} control positions of their own window; the first of them is Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:event}. Repeat epochs measured are the blocks at which the predicted stellar-frame cell was read, the remainder of them coming from the reserved hold-out and the archival extension. $T_{\rm  pers}$ is what a carrier persisting at the discovery amplitude $S$ would have given there and $T_{\rm  rep}$ is what is measured, combining every repeat epoch by inverse variance at the carried drift: one cell and one trial, so it may be negative, while the two indented readings beneath it are maxima and so positive on average whatever is there -- the second of them the same cell at every trial of the window's own drift grid, which bounds an error in the carried drift. The nearest block alone follows, because the combination asks the model of \S  \ref  {sec:etacrv} of every block across the whole baseline while the single reading asks it only of the two observations. The exclusion is the fraction of $S$ still ruled out at the trigger, $(T_{\rm  rep}+5)/T_{\rm  pers}$; the displacement permitted is half a channel in the star's frame. The last row but one is the chance $\Phi (5T_{\rm  pers}/T_\star -5)$ that a carrier at the trigger amplitude $5S/T_\star $ would have satisfied the criterion, and in brackets the same at $S$, which is selected as the largest of many cells and biased high.}}{19}{table.5.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cand}{{6}{19}{\textbf {The two events}, the crossings whose stellar statistic exceeds all \NCtrl {} control positions of their own window; the first of them is Fig.~\ref {fig:event}. Repeat epochs measured are the blocks at which the predicted stellar-frame cell was read, the remainder of them coming from the reserved hold-out and the archival extension. $T_{\rm pers}$ is what a carrier persisting at the discovery amplitude $S$ would have given there and $T_{\rm rep}$ is what is measured, combining every repeat epoch by inverse variance at the carried drift: one cell and one trial, so it may be negative, while the two indented readings beneath it are maxima and so positive on average whatever is there -- the second of them the same cell at every trial of the window's own drift grid, which bounds an error in the carried drift. The nearest block alone follows, because the combination asks the model of \S \ref {sec:etacrv} of every block across the whole baseline while the single reading asks it only of the two observations. The exclusion is the fraction of $S$ still ruled out at the trigger, $(T_{\rm rep}+5)/T_{\rm pers}$; the displacement permitted is half a channel in the star's frame. The last row but one is the chance $\Phi (5T_{\rm pers}/T_\star -5)$ that a carrier at the trigger amplitude $5S/T_\star $ would have satisfied the criterion, and in brackets the same at $S$, which is selected as the largest of many cells and biased high}{table.5.6}{}}
