\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {A sample of the crossing ledger, from which every disposition in this paper can be reproduced.} 9{} of the 56{} threshold crossings, one to a window: the 2{} unattributed crossings that lead their own control positions, the 3{} with no covering repeat and the one{} with no retained spectrum, and enough of the rest to show every disposition. \textbf  {The full ledger accompanies this paper as data rather than as part of the article}, machine-readable, with the execution block of each crossing, its rest-frame frequency and the recurrence readings this sample omits. The columns, the coded dispositions, the blanks and the dagger are defined in \S  \ref  {sec:ledgercolumns}.}}{34}{table.D.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ledger}{{D1}{34}{\textbf {A sample of the crossing ledger, from which every disposition in this paper can be reproduced.} \SiLdgSampN {} of the \EvNCrossRaw {} threshold crossings, one to a window: the \EvNRankQp {} unattributed crossings that lead their own control positions, the \MkAttrUntest {} with no covering repeat and the \CnNNoSpecWord {} with no retained spectrum, and enough of the rest to show every disposition. \textbf {The full ledger accompanies this paper as data rather than as part of the article}, machine-readable, with the execution block of each crossing, its rest-frame frequency and the recurrence readings this sample omits. The columns, the coded dispositions, the blanks and the dagger are defined in \S \ref {sec:ledgercolumns}}{table.D.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The recurrence stage's positive controls, and the baseline each was recovered over.} The 13{} line-attributed crossings the criterion of \S  \ref  {sec:summary} recovers, toward 2{} stars: for each, the block whose own crossing satisfies it at the same stellar-frame frequency, that block's epoch, its separation from the discovery block and the statistic it reaches there. Block identifiers omit the constant \texttt  {A002\_} prefix and are printed as session and block, so the first field is the observing session: every recovering block belongs to a different session from its discovery block and lies more than 1{} day from it, as the criterion demands. $T$ is the statistic of the recovering block's own crossing at that frequency; $T_{\rm  rep}$ beside it is the single-cell reading at the cell that crossing's own discovery predicts, taken in that same block through the frame correction, the start-of-block anchor and the carried drift, which is how the 33{} ledger exclusions are formed, and $\Delta c$ is that cell's distance in channels from where the block's own search puts the line. $\dagger $\nobreakspace  {}marks each reading of the one window whose surviving product peaks at 6.1886{} where 5.9557{} is published for the same cell, 3.9{} per cent apart. $\ddagger $\nobreakspace  {}marks a separation measured from an observing unit's stamp rather than from a block's own. Four{} of these crossings are present in blocks of their own observing unit as well, whose internal separations the archive does not record; the ledger file flags those rows in a column of its own.}}{35}{table.D.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:confirm}{{D2}{35}{\textbf {The recurrence stage's positive controls, and the baseline each was recovered over.} The \QcNCtl {} line-attributed crossings the criterion of \S \ref {sec:summary} recovers, toward \QcNStar {} stars: for each, the block whose own crossing satisfies it at the same stellar-frame frequency, that block's epoch, its separation from the discovery block and the statistic it reaches there. Block identifiers omit the constant \texttt {A002\_} prefix and are printed as session and block, so the first field is the observing session: every recovering block belongs to a different session from its discovery block and lies more than \EpSep {} day from it, as the criterion demands. $T$ is the statistic of the recovering block's own crossing at that frequency; $T_{\rm rep}$ beside it is the single-cell reading at the cell that crossing's own discovery predicts, taken in that same block through the frame correction, the start-of-block anchor and the carried drift, which is how the \RcNExcl {} ledger exclusions are formed, and $\Delta c$ is that cell's distance in channels from where the block's own search puts the line. $\dagger $~marks each reading of the one window whose surviving product peaks at \LrSuppOwn {} where \LrSuppPub {} is published for the same cell, \LrSuppPct {} per cent apart. $\ddagger $~marks a separation measured from an observing unit's stamp rather than from a block's own. \QcNUnrecWord {} of these crossings are present in blocks of their own observing unit as well, whose internal separations the archive does not record; the ledger file flags those rows in a column of its own}{table.D.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {F}Per-system sensitivity}{35}{appendix.F}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:persys}{{F}{35}{Per-system sensitivity}{appendix.F}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {F1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Per-system sensitivity of the carrier search.} For each of the 61{} systems the fine-channel search covers, the $\mathrm  {EIRP}_{90}$ of its best window (Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:quantities}), with that window's own primary-beam, annual-parallax and smearing retentions applied, beside that window's centre frequency $\nu $ and native channel width $\Delta \nu $ and the system's merged frequency coverage $\Delta \nu _{\rm  cov}$. Rows are ordered by the limit. \textbf  {Every limit here is nominal in one respect: it excludes atmospheric decorrelation}, and \emph  {dec.} is the percentage by which that leaves it optimistic -- measured from the phase calibrator of its own execution block, starred where the delivery exposes no phase record and the figure is the median of the blocks matched to it in baseline length, and dashed for $\epsilon $\nobreakspace  {}Eri, the one system whose blocks offer neither (\S  \ref  {sec:pninetysel}). It is reported and not folded in, so that a reader can apply it to the one limit in question; $\epsilon $\nobreakspace  {}Eri's window also omits the annual parallax and is optimistic by at most 9{} per cent on that account (Appendix\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {app:pbresp}). The last column records whether two execution blocks of different observing sessions cover a common frequency more than one day apart (d) or more than a year apart (yr), over the primary census together with every repeat execution block the recurrence test of \S  \ref  {sec:etacrv} reads -- 166{} blocks, of which 42{} lie outside the census (\S  \ref  {sec:confirmability} counts the systems each reaches). Both are lower bounds, since two blocks can be shown to be separated but never shown to be simultaneous. A gravitationally bound pair is one system and takes its primary's name. \textbf  {A dagger marks the 6{} systems whose own best window was one of the 49{} injected into, so that their completeness is measured}; the other 55{} limits are transferred from the class average and carry instead the dispersion observed between injected windows, $\times 0.80$ to $\times 1.58$, which is the interval that applies to one system's own limit and is wider than the calibration interval of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:p90budget}.}}{36}{table.F.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:persys}{{F1}{36}{\textbf {Per-system sensitivity of the carrier search.} For each of the \NSysClassA {} systems the fine-channel search covers, the $\mathrm {EIRP}_{90}$ of its best window (Table~\ref {tab:quantities}), with that window's own primary-beam, annual-parallax and smearing retentions applied, beside that window's centre frequency $\nu $ and native channel width $\Delta \nu $ and the system's merged frequency coverage $\Delta \nu _{\rm cov}$. Rows are ordered by the limit. \textbf {Every limit here is nominal in one respect: it excludes atmospheric decorrelation}, and \emph {dec.} is the percentage by which that leaves it optimistic -- measured from the phase calibrator of its own execution block, starred where the delivery exposes no phase record and the figure is the median of the blocks matched to it in baseline length, and dashed for $\epsilon $~Eri, the one system whose blocks offer neither (\S \ref {sec:pninetysel}). It is reported and not folded in, so that a reader can apply it to the one limit in question; $\epsilon $~Eri's window also omits the annual parallax and is optimistic by at most \BmEpsPxBoundPct {} per cent on that account (Appendix~\ref {app:pbresp}). The last column records whether two execution blocks of different observing sessions cover a common frequency more than one day apart (d) or more than a year apart (yr), over the primary census together with every repeat execution block the recurrence test of \S \ref {sec:etacrv} reads -- \ZcNRecEB {} blocks, of which \ZcNRecEBOut {} lie outside the census (\S \ref {sec:confirmability} counts the systems each reaches). Both are lower bounds, since two blocks can be shown to be separated but never shown to be simultaneous. A gravitationally bound pair is one system and takes its primary's name. \textbf {A dagger marks the \SmcNMeasSys {} systems whose own best window was one of the \SensNInj {} injected into, so that their completeness is measured}; the other \SmcNEstSys {} limits are transferred from the class average and carry instead the dispersion observed between injected windows, $\times \SmcDispLo $ to $\times \SmcDispHi $, which is the interval that applies to one system's own limit and is wider than the calibration interval of Table~\ref {tab:p90budget}}{table.F.1}{}}
